WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.885
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:02.885 --> 00:00:05.010
JESSICA CHOMIK-MORALES:
I'm Jessica Chomik-Morales,

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.850
and I'm a neuroscientist at
the McGovern Institute at MIT.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:12.000
I use a brain imaging technology
called Functional Magnetic

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.280
Resonance Imaging,
more commonly known

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:16.800
as fMRI, to see which
regions of the brain

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:19.680
become active when
we do certain things.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:21.540
Studying the brain
is a passion that

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.450
arose from being an only
child to immigrant parents.

00:00:24.450 --> 00:00:26.850
I grew up observing how
cultural differences can

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.280
drive differences in behavior.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.470
My family is from
Asuncion, Paraguay,

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:35.310
this really oddly shaped
country in South America.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.890
I'm producing a neuroscience
podcast in Spanish

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.900
to bring free and
valuable information

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.410
to the Hispanic community.

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.410
I know from my own
experience that there are not

00:00:43.410 --> 00:00:45.150
many Hispanic people
in the sciences.

00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.790
And on top of that,
most scientific content

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.650
is available only in English.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.740
But I'm going to change that.

00:00:52.740 --> 00:00:55.230
My podcast is called
Mi Última Neurona,

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:59.160
which translates to
"my last neuron."

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.080
I hope to inspire
the next generation

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.780
of Hispanic neuroscientists
with this podcast.

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

